const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Bioinformatics Service","serviceType":"Bioinformatics consulting, NGS data analysis, multi-omics integration and pipeline development","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/bioinformatics/","description":"NGS analysis with the reference build, tool versions and code recorded: RNA-seq, single-cell, variant calling, proteomics. From $100 a sample.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What bioinformatics tools, pipelines and databases do you use?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Open-source tools for almost everything, with licensed platforms where a project genuinely needs one. In practice that means STAR, HISAT2, Salmon, DESeq2, edgeR and limma for transcriptomics; Seurat, Scanpy and scVI for single cell; BWA-MEM2, GATK and DeepVariant for variant calling, with VEP or ANNOVAR and gnomAD and ClinVar for annotation; DADA2 and QIIME 2 against SILVA or GTDB for microbiome; MACS3 and the ENCODE quality metrics for chromatin; and FragPipe, MaxQuant or DIA-NN for proteomics, deposited through PRIDE. Pipelines are written in Nextflow or Snakemake with Singularity or Docker containers. Which tool is right depends on the data and the question, and in several places there is more than one defensible answer — we make the choice with you and record it rather than making it silently. We also say when a tool you are already using has been superseded: TopHat and Cufflinks are both in low-maintenance status and their own authors point users at HISAT2 and StringTie."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I know which bioinformatics package is right for my research?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Send the design and a computational biologist will tell you, at no cost and in two business days. What actually decides it is not the amount of data but how far the analysis has to travel: processing and describing a dataset is one kind of job, modelling differences between groups with the design handled properly is another, and building a pipeline your team will run repeatedly is a third. A useful test is what the deliverable has to survive. If it is going into a thesis chapter, the primary analysis is usually enough. If it is going to a journal with a data availability policy, you need the deposit package and the code as well. If it is going into a regulatory submission or a clinical decision, the documentation is most of the work and should be scoped that way from the start."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you ensure compliance with international guidelines and journal standards?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By working to the standard that actually governs the deliverable, which differs by assay. Data handling and deposit follow the FAIR principles, with MIAME for microarray work, MINSEQE for sequencing, MIxS for environmental and microbiome data and MIAPE for proteomics — these are what repositories enforce at deposit. Variant discovery follows GATK Best Practices or the documented equivalent for the caller used. Where the deliverable is a predictive model, reporting follows TRIPOD+AI; where it is a diagnostic accuracy claim, STARD; where it is a prognostic biomarker, REMARK. Clinical and laboratory work sits under GCP and GLP where applicable, and human data under GDPR, HIPAA and, for NIH-funded work, the Genomic Data Sharing Policy. Where the analysis supports a regulatory submission, the expectations of the FDA, the EMA or the equivalent authority govern the documentation. Publication ethics and authorship follow ICMJE and COPE. One point worth making plainly: CONSORT is a reporting guideline for randomised trials and does not govern a bioinformatics workflow, so we do not claim it does."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you integrate bioinformatics results with experimental or clinical data?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and the honest part of the answer is that integration is harder than it looks and frequently less informative than a well-run single-layer analysis. Combining genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, metabolomics and epigenomics on shared samples can find mechanism that no single layer shows, but only where the samples really are shared, the sample sizes support it and the layers were not each processed in different batches. Where those conditions hold we use factor-based and network methods and report which layer is carrying the signal rather than presenting one combined figure that conceals it. Where they do not hold we will say so. Integrating with clinical or phenotype data brings its own problem, which is that clinical variables are usually incomplete and the missingness is rarely random — so how missing data was handled becomes part of the result rather than a footnote to it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the typical turnaround time for a bioinformatics project?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A small dataset with a standard design is usually one to two weeks; a medium project with modelling and interpretation is three to four; and a large or multi-omics analysis is five to eight, with priority delivery available. Those are the compute-and-analysis figures and they are the easy part to predict. What actually moves a timeline is rarely the computation: it is metadata that has to be reconstructed because the sample sheet is incomplete, access approvals for controlled data, or a quality problem that surfaces at the first pass and needs a decision from you before anything else can proceed. We flag all three at scoping, and if a date is genuinely at risk you hear it early with what can be done about it rather than on the day."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you ensure confidentiality and data security?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A non-disclosure agreement is signed before any material moves — yours if you have one, ours if you prefer — and writers, analysts and project staff are bound by internal confidentiality agreements as well. Data is held in encrypted, access-controlled environments under a quality management system certified to ISO 9001:2015, processed under GDPR and HIPAA-compliant handling, kept only as long as the project needs it and then deleted on your instruction. Nothing is used to train any model and nothing is shared outside your project team. The specific point about genomic data is worth stating: a human genome is identifying and cannot be fully anonymised, so it is treated as identifying throughout rather than as de-identified once the name is removed, and controlled-access deposit is planned at design rather than at submission."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you provide post-project support?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is the part clients use most. Reviewers come back asking for an extra covariate, a different model or a sensitivity analysis, and because the pipeline, the containers and the seeds ship with the report, running those is usually an afternoon rather than a restart. New samples arrive and get folded into the existing analysis with the batch handled properly. Figures get rebuilt to a different journal’s specification. And where a tool that produced your results has been superseded, we will tell you whether rerunning changes the conclusion — which it often does not, and saying so is more useful than a rerun for its own sake."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many replicates do I need, and at what sequencing depth?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Replicates almost always matter more than depth, and the instinct to buy depth instead is the commonest way to waste a sequencing budget. For bulk differential expression, most designs are better served by more biological replicates at moderate depth than by fewer at high depth, because the variance you are fighting is between individuals rather than between reads. For single cell, what matters is how many samples you have rather than how many cells: cells within a sample are not independent replicates, so a study with forty thousand cells from three people has three replicates. For variant calling the constraint is different again and depth genuinely governs sensitivity, particularly for low-frequency variants. The right answer depends on the effect size you care about and the variability in your system, so we work it out from pilot data or published estimates and tell you what the design can detect — including when the answer is that it cannot detect what you are hoping for."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"A reviewer has questioned our analysis. Can you tell us whether they are right?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and that is a distinct service from rerunning it. The first step is reconstructing what the original analysis actually did, which is straightforward if code and versions exist and is itself most of the work if they do not. Then the objection is assessed on its merits. Roughly speaking they fall into three groups: objections that are right and require a rerun; objections that are right in principle but that a sensitivity analysis can answer without redoing anything; and objections where the reviewer has misread the methods, which is usually a writing problem and is the most fixable of the three. Knowing which you have within a week is worth considerably more than assuming the worst for a month. Where the objection is right we say so plainly, including when that means the original conclusion does not survive."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What if the analysis comes back null?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Then that is the result, and we will help you report it properly rather than looking for a comparison that reaches significance. There is a real difference between a study that found no effect and a study that could not have found one, and establishing which you have is the useful next step: if the design was underpowered for the effect size that matters, that is worth knowing before you plan the next experiment, and it is a legitimate and publishable finding in its own right. What we will not do is run comparisons until something is significant and then present that comparison as though it had been planned, or move a threshold after seeing what it returns. Those produce a result that will not replicate, and the cost of that arrives later and lands on you rather than on us."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you need our raw data, or can you work from processed files?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Either, with a caveat worth knowing. Working from raw data — FASTQ, BCL, raw mass spectra — means we control every decision from quality control onwards and can record all of them, which is what makes the analysis reproducible and the methods section writable. Working from a processed count matrix or a peak table is faster and cheaper, and it means inheriting somebody else’s undocumented choices about trimming, alignment, normalisation and filtering. We will work from processed files, and we will state in the report which decisions were made before the data reached us and which of them we could not verify. That sentence protects you at review."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you help us deposit data to GEO, SRA, ENA or PRIDE?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is worth starting earlier than most groups do. Deposit is not a single upload; it is a metadata exercise governed by community standards — MINSEQE for sequencing, MIAME-derived expectations for arrays, the PRIDE requirements for proteomics — and the part that takes time is assembling sample-level metadata that nobody recorded consistently. A journal with a data availability policy will hold acceptance until an accession exists, and an accession issued under embargo still has to be issued. We prepare the submission alongside the analysis rather than after it, so the accession is in hand when the manuscript goes out."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What compute do you use, and can you run on our infrastructure?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Pipelines are written in Nextflow or Snakemake with Singularity or Docker containers, which means they are portable by construction: the same workflow runs on our infrastructure, on your institutional cluster, or in your cloud account. Running in your environment is the right choice when the data cannot leave it — patient-identifiable sequence data under HIPAA or GDPR, or data governed by a controlled-access agreement — and we work that way regularly. You receive the workflow, the container image and the parameter files whichever route is taken, so the analysis does not depend on us being available to re-run it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you reproduce an analysis somebody else ran for us?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Often, and the attempt itself is informative. Where the code, the reference build, the annotation release and the parameters were recorded, reproduction is mechanical and usually confirms the result. Where they were not, the honest outcome is a range: this is what we get under the most plausible reconstruction, and here is how much the answer moves under the alternatives. That range is itself the finding, and it is the answer to give a reviewer who has asked whether the result is robust. We have also several times found that the original result depended on a decision nobody had written down, which is a difficult conversation and a better one to have before publication."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you handle single-cell data where cells are not independent?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By not treating cells as replicates, which is the most consequential error in the field. Thousands of cells from three mice give you precision about those three mice, not about the population; the experimental unit is the donor, and a differential expression test run across cells will return hundreds of confidently significant genes that do not replicate. The defensible approach is pseudobulk aggregation to the sample level followed by a standard differential expression framework, or a mixed model with a random effect for donor. We say which was used and why, and where a published analysis treated cells as independent we will tell you what that does to the claim."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Bioinformatics","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/bioinformatics/"}]}];

